import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as s,y as o,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.04 Systems, Control, Feedback, Resilience, and Time Lags","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.04 Systems, Control, Feedback, Resilience, and Time Lags","xkChars":0,"xkWords":524,"xkLessons":{"D.04":{"title":"D.04 Systems, Control, Feedback, Resilience, and Delays","lead":"Knowing the network diagram of a system does not mean knowing its responses; knowing each task can be done separately does not mean all can be completed together. Systems analysis must place observability, reachability, resources, deadlines, and authority within the same actual task.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5028–5043; source anchor section-d-04; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “Investigation, scheduling, and feedback should serve strategies that can actually be executed.” An exact source excerpt is separately attached in source-slice.md; explanation and source text remain separate.","System representation connects stocks, flows, feedback, delays, constraints, and boundaries to specific objects. A stock is an accumulated quantity at a point in time; a flow is inflow or outflow per unit time; feedback is a returning effect influencing subsequent states; a delay is the time a channel or response requires; constraints limit available actions; boundaries specify what the analysis includes. Variables and units must be proportionate to the actual task; drawing arrows alone does not establish a mechanism.","Feedback requires only that a returning influence affect subsequent states; it does not guarantee truthful, timely, or beneficial information. False indicators can cause later misallocation and still constitute a returning effect. Receiving correct feedback without changing subsequent states does not yet establish that the corresponding feedback loop is connected. An organization learning a practice, an emergent pattern appearing, insufficiency of the current representation, and cross-scale causality holding are four different judgments. Unpredictable patterns cannot all be called learning, nor can representation failure automatically be elevated into higher-level causality.","Observability must be relative to the target. Under linear observation O and target L, if every unobservable difference leaves the target unchanged—that is, ker(O)⊆ker(L)—observations identify Lx without reconstructing all states. The public argument is: if Ox=Ox′, then O(x−x′)=0; inclusion gives L(x−x′)=0, hence Lx=Lx′. Conversely, if some v satisfies Ov=0 but Lv≠0, x and x+v have identical observations but different targets, so the observation cannot uniquely identify the target. This result rests on assumptions such as linearity and a specified state domain; real-world mappings still need checking.","The rank of a controllability matrix establishes only a particular reachability property in an ideal model. Actual execution remains constrained by time, input magnitude, resources, lawfulness of actions, and authority. Even if a one-dimensional system's control direction spans its entire state space, a single time step with input bounded by 1 cannot take it from 0 to 2. Rank and qualifications to act operate at different levels; mathematically allowed infinite inputs cannot be treated as actually available resources.","The same network diagram with different response rules can produce different propagation. Nodes may change after one contact, or require multiple neighbors, a specific sequence, or sufficient resources. Delays combined with deadlines, forgetting, and depletion may terminate propagation. In special models with infinite waiting, monotonic accumulation, and no forgetting, finite delays may change only arrival times, not final coverage. Neither result can be generalized into the other; first state the dynamic rules and observation endpoint.","Resilience concerns maintenance or recovery of a relation or function under disturbance, but what is maintained may be an unjust institution. Successful maintenance cannot directly establish justification, nor can restoring the original state be assumed as every person's life goal. People may wish to exit an old structure or change distribution instead of merely restoring it. Function, values, rights, and action choices still need separate explanation.","Stock models help distinguish temporary flow balance from sustainable capacity. A falling backlog may arise from reduced demand, increased capability, changed measurement definitions, or outsourcing; a curve alone cannot distinguish them. Temporarily increasing throughput by overextending personnel does not establish greater long-term capacity either. Short-term overshoot may be compatible with several delays, response strengths, or external disturbances; a single curve cannot directly identify every delay.","When history is sufficiently encoded in the current state, general adaptation and learning may still occur, and carrier-based feedback-mediated learning may hold. Historical-increment learning separately asks whether history adds information or capability beyond the specified current representation. No extra historical increment cannot mean no learning of any kind, and one behavioral change cannot automatically be recorded as meeting every formal condition for learning.","Joint feasibility is stronger than separate feasibility. Two tasks due in the same period, each requiring one unit of capacity, are individually feasible but cannot both be completed when total capacity for that period is one. Add genuinely available capacity or lawfully change timing; the same resource cannot be allocated twice on paper. Furthermore, when identical observations correspond to several states, each state having a correct action does not ensure a shared action is available without knowing the true state. Executable strategies must depend on information actually obtainable.","The twelve topics cover system representation, control and feedback, observer effects, complex adaptation and emergence, network cascades, resilience, historical lock-in, organizational ecology, observational blind spots, intervention capabilities, regulators and internal models, and stocks, delays, and overshoot. Listed figures such as Bertalanffy, Meadows, Wiener, Ashby, Holling, and Arthur provide research navigation, not an assertion that they agree completely or that this section reconstructs all original proofs. The strongest objection is that real-world restrictions render formal analysis useless. Formal analysis can still exclude impossible inferences and clarify conditions needing supplementation; it simply cannot cross its model boundaries to serve as real-world authorization."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Feedback","leftText":"A returning effect influences subsequent states.","rightTitle":"Beneficial feedback or learning","rightText":"Truth, timeliness, effects, and the conditions for learning all require separate checks."},{"leftTitle":"Target identifiable","leftText":"Observations suffice to determine the Lx asked for by the task.","rightTitle":"Full state reconstructible","rightText":"A stronger requirement; target-irrelevant directions may not need observation."},{"leftTitle":"Ideal controllability","leftText":"Reachability under the model's input and time assumptions.","rightTitle":"Real-world executability","rightText":"Additionally requires bounds, resources, authority, time windows, stopping, and remedy."},{"leftTitle":"Separate feasibility","leftText":"Each task individually satisfies resource conditions.","rightTitle":"Joint feasibility","rightText":"Overall constraints remain satisfied when tasks compete for resources simultaneously."},{"leftTitle":"Resilience","leftText":"Maintenance of a function or relation.","rightTitle":"Justified and worth restoring","rightText":"Requires normative reasons and the circumstances of affected people; stability cannot establish it."}],"boundaries":["ker(O)⊆ker(L) is a condition for linear target identification; it does not directly encompass arbitrary nonlinear or real-world objects.","Delays leave final coverage unchanged only under explicit conditions such as infinite waiting, monotonic accumulation, and no forgetting.","Stock and overshoot curves cannot independently identify actual channels; resource transfers and overextension need checking.","Each state having a correct action does not imply a shared usable strategy under identical observations.","All mathematical examples in this lesson are conditional constructions; no actual control system was operated or historical spaceflight experiment replicated."],"questions":["If O=(1,0) cannot see x2, can it calculate 2x1?","Why can the full-rank example not reach 2 in one step?","Does a declining backlog establish increased capacity?","When does delay leave final coverage unchanged?","Does strong resilience establish that an institution should continue?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-04/figures/section-d-04.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual original test record: Apollo13's emergency filter adaptation faced material and time constraints","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19720021432/downloads/19720021432.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual original test record: Apollo13's emergency filter adaptation faced material and time constraints","text":"NASA report CSD-A-1066 explains that the emergency adaptation during Apollo13 had to work, use materials available to the crew, be easy to communicate clearly, and be evaluated early enough to prepare an alternative. The report records unoccupied-chamber tests injecting carbon dioxide to simulate crew metabolism; the second phase determined canister replacement times to reduce electricity consumption from reliance on telemetry for that purpose. This brings actual materials, communicable procedures, deadlines, observation, and power constraints into the same task.","sourceLabel":"NASA CSD-A-1066 original ground-test report, recording April 1970 tests","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19720021432/downloads/19720021432.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed p3 Objective and first Discussion paragraph; p4 final sentence of the first paragraph and final paragraph (second phase, 1970-04-16); p5 Conclusions; original scans on PDF pages 6–8","boundary":"Only these three body pages were checked, with visual inspection of scans. No claim is made to repeat the tests, review all appendix data, or establish resolution of every spaceflight-system risk; report values are not used to guide actual life-support operations.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19720021432/downloads/19720021432.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.04","start_line":5084,"end_line":5099,"startLine":5084,"endLine":5099}],"outputId":"section-d-04","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["First connect system variables, flow units, boundaries, and response rules to the specific task.","Test whether observations distinguish necessary states for the target; do not demand irrelevant full-state reconstruction.","Check ideal reachability separately from actual deadlines, input bounds, resources, and authority.","Check dynamic rules, delays, and endpoints before explaining propagation, stocks, and resilience; avoid inferring mechanisms from diagrams.","Check joint resource constraints and shared strategies under identical observations; investigation and scheduling should serve actual executability."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: not all states need to be visible","text":"Let x=(x1,x2), observation O=(1,0), and target L=(2,0). The invisible directions are (0,t), all of which leave L unchanged. Observation z=x1 therefore suffices to obtain target 2z, while x2 remains unknown. If the target changes to L′=(0,1), then (0,0) and (0,1) have the same observation but different targets, making identification impossible.","assumptions":"The state space is R²; observation and target are linear and noise-free.","boundary":"Actual measurement errors and target definitions require separate checks; the example cannot justify ignoring critical states."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: full rank can still be too late","text":"For one-dimensional dynamics x at the next step=x+u, the control matrix is [1] with rank 1. Starting at 0 with target 2, if only one step is available and |u|≤1, the reachable interval is [−1,1], so 2 is unreachable. If two steps are allowed with u=1 each, reaching 2 is formally possible, but real-world authority and resources are still not established.","assumptions":"A deterministic discrete model without external disturbances, with explicit time steps and magnitude bounds.","boundary":"No actual equipment capability is estimated."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: joint tasks and hidden states","text":"Two tasks with the same deadline each require capacity 1, while total capacity is 1. Each is separately feasible, but their combined need is 2>1. Capacity rising to 2 or a lawful staggering of time windows changes the conditions. Separately, suppose state A succeeds only under action a and state B only under b, but both states have identical observations and a≠b. Without new information, no single action is correct for both.","assumptions":"Action sets and success conditions are teaching constructions; the latter example permits only actions a and b.","boundary":"In reality, safe pausing or investigation may be studied separately, but cannot be assumed without grounds to be costless or always feasible."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: identical connections, different delay outcomes","text":"Suppose there is only one edge A→B. An event leaves A at time 0 and reaches B after delay 2. If B must receive it before time 1, propagation fails; if infinite waiting is allowed and arrival permanently activates B, activation occurs at time 2 and final coverage remains A and B.","assumptions":"Finite positive delays, with no forgetting, depletion, or other edges; the two situations differ only in their deadline conditions.","boundary":"This does not assert that all real-world delays affect only speed or necessarily terminate."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The feedback returned, so the system must have learned the correct action.","effect":"Returning effects, truthfulness, beneficialness, and learning each require evidence.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The control matrix has full rank, so there is authority to complete the target action immediately.","effect":"Rank provides neither time and resources nor real-world authorization; the amplitude-limited example already refutes this inference.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Every state has a correct action, so correct action is possible under any information conditions.","effect":"When information cannot distinguish states, the intersection of feasible actions may be empty.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If O=(1,0) cannot see x2, can it calculate 2x1?","answer":"Yes, because the invisible direction does not change the target; reconstructing the irrelevant x2 is unnecessary.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can the full-rank example not reach 2 in one step?","answer":"The implemented input bound permits an increase or decrease of only 1 in one step; formal coverage of directions does not mean arbitrary magnitudes are available.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a declining backlog establish increased capacity?","answer":"Not necessarily. Changes in demand, outsourcing, or statistical boundaries could also produce it; channel material is needed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When does delay leave final coverage unchanged?","answer":"Only when the relevant conditions of the specific model hold, such as unlimited waiting and monotonic accumulation without forgetting, and all necessary arrivals still occur.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does strong resilience establish that an institution should continue?","answer":"It does not; maintenance of function and normative justification require separate judgments.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-04/figures/section-d-04.png","alt":"Tasks and representations; stocks / flows / feedback; delays / constraints / boundaries; target observability: ker(O) ⊆ ker(L), without reconstructing the whole state; ideal controllability requires separate checks of time limits / amplitude / resources, permissions / actual reachability; identical graphs ≠ identical dynamics, response rules determine propagation and delay conditions must be specified; individual feasibility ≠ joint feasibility, identical observations may admit no common action; feedback ≠ benefit or learning, resilience ≠ normative justification. Drawn by the author based on D.04; conditional formal examples do not certify the control capabilities of actual devices or organizations.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on D.04; conditional formal examples do not certify the control capabilities of actual devices or organizations."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Switch the target L, input limit, task deadline, and observational discrimination to show formal identification and strategy-feasibility conditions separately.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An original ground-test report shows how available materials, time, and resources constrain an implementation plan; one emergency success is not treated as proof of all feedback or resilience claims."},"openQuestions":["Not all original research manuscripts for 04.01–04.12 were obtained; their complete theoretical derivations and historical adoption are not reconstructed."],"taskId":"S-D-04","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 4577–4592 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["D.04: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"apollo13-lioh","title":"CSD-A-1066 Final Report – Apollo 13 LiOH Canister Test","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19720021432/downloads/19720021432.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"CSD-A-1066; describes tests on 1970-04-14 and 04-16; NTRS archive identifier 19720021432","locator":"Printed pp3–5; Objective, opening paragraph of Discussion, phase-two paragraph on p4, and Conclusions; original scans on PDF pages 6–8 were visually inspected","claim_support":["The modification needed to work, use available materials, be easy to communicate, and be assessed in time; unmanned chamber simulation; testing determined replacement intervals to reduce telemetry power consumption"],"limits":["Only three pages of the report's main text were checked. Tests were not repeated, and neither appendix curves nor unchecked numerical values elsewhere in the report were used for conclusions."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.04　系统、控制、反馈、韧性与时滞; Chinese source lines 5028–5043; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-04/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-04/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-04/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-04/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"9af75e6cea97bed055d07afdd8703ce573b6b47765d2b4cef202e7d86513aa55","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-04.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-04.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-04.md"};function d(u,e,h,p,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o("",1)),r(t,{section:"D.04"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=a(l,[["render",d]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
